  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSatellite imagery is showing clear skies across eastern NY and \nwestern New England, with ridging from high pressure over eastern \nCanada extending across our region. Broad N'ly flow is keeping \ntemperatures below average throughout this afternoon, which will\ncontinue into this evening. The aforementioned high will drift \nSE into New England overnight, with low temperatures dropping \ninto the mid-20s to low 30s. This will be accompanied by an \nincrease in clouds by early Wednesday morning associated with an\napproaching warm front from the south. Closer to average \ntemperatures are expected on Wednesday, with highs staying in \nthe 50s to near 60. SW'ly flow will dominate on Thursday as the \nhigh moves offshore New England, advecting in an anomously warm \nair mass and bringing the return of above average temperatures \n(around 5-10 degrees above normal). A cold front with minimal \nmoisture is expected to pass through the region from the NW \nThursday night into Friday, with a few short-lived showers \nexpected in the Adirondacks. Above average temperatures will \npersist through the day on Friday, with winds strengthening \nbehind the front in the afternoon and evening. Another high \npressure system will approach the region over the weekend, \ncontinuing the trend of above average temperatures and dry \nweather. The next chance for rain is looking to be early next \nweek per latest guidance, but there is still a large model \nspread so confidence is low regarding timing and amounts.",
